※ pl4g-test: compile=ok opt=0,1 run=native,qemu exit=0 diag=none

※ A comparison of floating-point values, standing where a condition stands.
※
※ Every other comparison is absorbed by the branch that reads it: the machine
※ compares and then branches on what the comparison wrote, so there is no truth
※ value anywhere and nothing to hold it.  A floating-point comparison is not.
※ What these machines write for one is a different set of flags, on which a
※ value that is not a number is a fourth answer and takes more than one reading
※ to tell from the other three -- so the comparison is computed into a register
※ and the branch tests that register.
※
※ All three selectors said so and the one function all three ask did not, so a
※ floating-point comparison written as a condition was refused by the code
※ generator and the same comparison handed to a function was not.  Which is why
※ this is here: what it is about is the *place* the comparison stands in, and
※ nothing before it tested that place.

@[startup, impure]
fn main() → u8:
    let a: f64 = 1.0f64
    let b: f64 = 2.0f64
    let n: f32 = 1.0f32
    let m: f32 = 2.0f32
    ※ the exact orderings
    if ¬(a < b) ∨ a > b ∨ ¬(a ≤ b) ∨ a ≥ b:
        1u8
    ※ the same in the narrower format
    elif ¬(n < m) ∨ n > m:
        2u8
    ※ the approximate six, which are a subtraction and one of these
    elif a ≅ b ∨ ¬(a ≇ b):
        3u8
    elif ¬(a ⪅ b) ∨ a ⪆ b ∨ ¬(a ⪉ b) ∨ a ⪊ b:
        4u8
    ※ one that is true of a value and itself, where the two sides are the one
    ※ value and no flag reading is in doubt
    elif ¬(a ≅ a) ∨ ¬(a ⪅ a) ∨ ¬(a ⪆ a):
        5u8
    ※ a comparison of what two operations came to, so that the operands are in
    ※ registers rather than written down
    elif ¬(a + b < b × b):
        6u8
    ※ and the same comparison read as a value, which is the path that worked
    ※ all along and has to go on working
    elif told(a < b) ≠ 1u8:
        7u8
    else:
        0u8

fn told(answer: bool) → u8:
    if answer:
        1u8
    else:
        0u8
